      "created_at": "2026-07-07T18:39:19Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-07-07T18:39:19Z",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "I was bitten by this as well on signet with Core 31.0.0.\n\nThis was at height [312173](https://mempool.space/signet/block/0000000862ad56e308f949baf525228f0987522c82ea01d85045543236cc64a9).\n\nIn my case, `estimatesmartfee 6 ECONOMICAL` returned about `0.19369611 BTC/kvB` (~19k sat/vB) while `mempoolminfee`, `minrelaytxfee`, and `incrementalrelayfee` were all `0.00000100 BTC/kvB`.\n\nThe raw breakdown seems to show `estimatesmartfee 6` being dominated by Core's internal `estimaterawfee 3 0.6` component:\n\n```\nestimaterawfee 3 0.6\n  short.feerate: 0.19399130 BTC/kvB\n  short.pass.startrange: 9538614 sat/kvB\n  short.pass.endrange: inf\n  short.pass.totalconfirmed: 19.59\n  short.pass.inmempool: 10\n\nestimaterawfee 6 0.85\n  medium.feerate: 0.00003381 BTC/kvB\n\nestimaterawfee 12 0.95\n  long.feerate: 0.00001828 BTC/kvB\n```\n\nSo the 6-block smart estimate is getting pulled up by the 3-block/60% short bucket, not by the 6- or 12-block components.\n\nOne possibly useful version boundary: a Core 26.0rc3 signet node returned the expected floor-ish result for the same high-level query:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"feerate\": 0.00001000,\n  \"blocks\": 6\n}\n```\n\nThat makes me wonder whether the behavior changed around v27+, possibly related to #21161 / commit a5e39d325d (\"Fee estimation: extend bucket ranges consistently\"). Not saying that is definitely the culprit, but it looks like a useful bisect point.\n",
